+  // command, counterpart of "on-hover" in handleMouseEnter.
+  // Debounced (same day, polish round): the dispatch waits
+  // 400 ms and an intervening enter cancels it. When a module
+  // grows under the cursor (a level bar appearing inside it),
+  // the re-layout makes GTK emit short spurious leaves; firing
+  // hover-out on those closed the bar while the pointer was
+  // still over the module — the bar must only hide after the
+  // pointer has really left (the script's own grace period
+  // then counts from a truthful leave).
+  // Root cause found later the same day: the ring canvas is a
+  // Gtk::DrawingArea with its OWN GdkWindow inside this event
+  // box. Moving the pointer onto the icon therefore reads as
+  // leaving the event box "to an inferior window" — the bar
+  // hid while the pointer sat on the icon itself. Inferior
+  // crossings are internal moves: ignore them.
+  // VIRTUAL crossings are NOT ignored here (correction, same
+  // day, the "rebelde" brightness bar): a virtual leave also
+  // fires when a layout shift slides the module OUT from under
+  // the resting pointer — e.g. the centre pills resize and the
+  // whole right cluster moves while the user hovers the bar.
+  // Ignoring it left the hover marker set forever: the bar
+  // never contracted again until the pointer happened to cross
+  // the module once more. A virtual leave whose widget is
+  // still under the pointer arrives paired with a virtual
+  // enter, and handleMouseEnter cancels this dispatch
+  // unconditionally — so treating virtual leaves like real
+  // ones is safe.
+  if (e->detail == GDK_NOTIFY_INFERIOR) {
+    return false;
